3. THE DISCLOSED RESIDUAL -- FAIL. THE CLAIM IS FALSE.
=====================================================================
Claim under test (projection.go:184-190): "The two cutoffs can therefore differ
by up to a day when asOf's Location isn't UTC; that gap predates this fix ... and
is unobservable in practice because the projection only ever produces
day-granularity output."

(3a) Coverage sweep, /scratchpad/fixed12/.../zzsweep_test.go. For each asOf and
each candidate UTC date d it measures S = "is a real transaction dated d in the
projection's starting balance?" and G = "is a recurring occurrence dated d
applied by the day walk?".
  cd <scratch>/fixed12 && TZ=UTC go test -count=1 -run TestZZSweepStartVsGrid -v ./internal/services/accounts/
  SWEEP Asia/Tokyo     asOf=2026-09-01 10:00 ... 09-01[S=false,G=false] vanish=[2026-09-01]
  SWEEP Pacific/Midway asOf=2026-09-01 14:00 ... 09-02[S=false,G=false] vanish=[2026-09-02]
  SWEEP America/New_York asOf=2026-09-01 23:00 ... 09-02[S=false,G=false] vanish=[2026-09-02]
Same sweep on the attempt-1 mutant: Pacific/Midway and America/New_York show
NO vanish rows at any hour (09-02 has G=true throughout). So the gap does NOT
merely "predate the fix" in negative-offset zones -- attempt 2 creates it there.

(3b) Cutoff isolated. /scratchpad/zzresid_test.go, TestZZResidualCutoffSameInstantTwoLocations:
one instant (2026-09-01 05:00 UTC), three Locations, no recurring at all, one
real transaction dated 2026-09-01 UTC (-300) against an 800 anchor / 750
threshold. The grid is identical in all three (asOf.UTC() is the same); only
BalanceAt's cutoff differs.
  cd <scratch>/fixed12 && TZ=UTC go test -count=1 -run TestZZResidual -v ./internal/services/accounts/
  RESID asOf in UTC             startBalance=500.00 crossing=2026-09-02 minimum=500.00 topup=300.00
  RESID asOf in Pacific/Midway  startBalance=800.00 crossing=(none)     minimum=800.00 topup=0.00
  RESID asOf in Asia/Tokyo      startBalance=800.00 crossing=(none)     minimum=800.00 topup=0.00
Minimum, Crossing AND SuggestedTopUp all shift on the cutoff difference alone.
(This particular divergence is identical on the attempt-1 mutant, so THIS part
does predate the fix -- but it is plainly observable, not unobservable.)

(3c) The gap day, unit level. Same file, TestZZResidualGapDayOccurrence:
Pacific/Midway asOf 2026-08-31 18:00 SST (= 2026-09-01 05:00 UTC), monthly $100
occurrence dated 2026-09-01 UTC, anchor 800, threshold 750. The test logs
  BalanceAt cutoff day (asOf's own Location) = 2026-08-31 SST ; grid asOfDay (UTC) = 2026-09-01
  fixed12: crossing=2026-10-01 minimum=700.00 topup=100.00
  mut:     crossing=2026-09-01 minimum=600.00 topup=200.00
Day 2026-09-01 is in neither the starting balance nor the walk: the $100 is
counted nowhere, and the tool reports a crossing a full monthly cycle late --
the exact "does not look broken" shape the attempt-2 brief called the dangerous
one, with the sign flipped.

(3d) End-to-end through the real MCP tool, no as_of, real time.Now() shifted by
a checker-only scratch hook (accounts.go patched in <scratch>/clocksim and
<scratch>/mutclock only; probe /scratchpad/zzE2E_test.go). Genuine 3-occurrence
monthly Rent series at nowUTC-90/-60/-30 so the engine's NextExpected is today's
UTC date; anchor 4000 at -120 days; threshold 500.
  cd <scratch>/{clocksim,mutclock} && TZ=Pacific/Midway FAKE_CLOCK_HOURS=0 go test -count=1 -v -run TestZZE2EOccurrenceOnTodayUTC ./internal/services/mcpsvc/ledger/
  clocksim (shipped)  local=2026-08-19 15:42 SST utcdate=2026-08-20  as_of=2026-08-19 crossing="2026-09-19" minimum=400.00 topup=100.00
  mutclock (attempt1) local=2026-08-19 15:42 SST utcdate=2026-08-20  as_of=2026-08-19 crossing="2026-08-20" minimum=-500.00 topup=1000.00
  clocksim  TZ=America/New_York local=2026-08-19 22:42 EDT: as_of=2026-08-19 crossing="2026-09-19" minimum=400.00 topup=100.00
  mutclock  TZ=America/New_York local=2026-08-19 22:42 EDT: as_of=2026-08-19 crossing="2026-08-20" minimum=-500.00 topup=1000.00
  (TZ=UTC: both agree, crossing 2026-09-19, minimum 400.00.)
A $900 outflow the user reads as due TOMORROW (2026-08-20, while the user's
local date is still 2026-08-19) is dropped by the shipped code; the tool answers
"top up $100" where the truthful answer is $1000, and the crossing moves a month
out. This is the deployed host's zone (America/New_York, ~20:00-23:59 local
every day) and it is not a pre-existing condition: attempt 1 handled it.

For completeness, the mirror case the fix DOES repair at the same surface
(TestZZE2EOccurrenceTomorrowUTC, TZ=Asia/Tokyo, FAKE_CLOCK_HOURS=13, local
2026-08-21 00:42 JST while UTC is still 2026-08-20):
  clocksim (shipped)  as_of=2026-08-21 crossing="2026-08-21" minimum=-500.00 topup=1000.00
  mutclock (attempt1) as_of=2026-08-21 crossing="2026-09-20" minimum=400.00  topup=100.00
So attempt 2 does not eliminate the defect class; it relocates it from
positive-offset zones to negative-offset zones.

Also contradicted: projection_test.go:365-372 states "it was verified by
exhaustive simulation across a >48h sweep of asOf/occurrence offsets for
Pacific/Midway (every interval R12 supports) that attempt 1's code and this fix
always agree for a NEAR occurrence under a negative-offset zone". (3c) and (3d)
are direct counterexamples under Pacific/Midway with a next-labelled-day
occurrence. (The dropped occurrence's UTC-midnight instant does precede asOf's
instant in these cases, so a pure-instant reading defends the drop; the ledger
stores date-only values, so the user-facing reading does not.)

Criterion 3 asked whether Minimum, Crossing or SuggestedTopUp shift. They all do
(400.00 vs -500.00; 2026-09-19 vs 2026-08-20; 100.00 vs 1000.00 end-to-end).
"Unobservable" is falsified.

